WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.792
Human approval, done properly.

2
00:00:02.072 --> 00:00:17.645
The Model Context Protocol specification, which defines how agent applications connect to tool servers, says there should always be a human in the loop with the ability to deny tool invocations, and that applications should show which tools are exposed,

3
00:00:17.925 --> 00:00:22.491
indicate clearly when tools run, and ask for confirmation on operations.

4
00:00:22.771 --> 00:00:31.347
OWASP calls the absence of this “excessive autonomy”: an application that fails to independently verify and approve high-impact actions.

5
00:00:31.627 --> 00:00:35.723
Four details separate a real approval gate from a decorative one.

6
00:00:36.003 --> 00:00:42.424
First, deny by default: if the approval callback is missing, broken, or times out, nothing is written.

7
00:00:42.704 --> 00:00:51.557
Second, validate before you ask: run your schema and semantic checks first, so a person is never asked to approve a call that would fail anyway.

8
00:00:51.837 --> 00:01:00.008
Third, show the exact arguments, not the model’s summary of them; the summary is model output too, and could be wrong or manipulated.

9
00:01:00.288 --> 00:01:11.019
Fourth, tell the model the outcome: a declined call returns an is_error result saying it was declined and should not be retried, so the loop moves on instead of asking again.

10
00:01:11.299 --> 00:01:14.029
Approval has a cost, and that shapes the design.

11
00:01:14.309 --> 00:01:18.875
If people are asked to approve every lookup, they will start clicking yes without reading.

12
00:01:19.155 --> 00:01:32.211
Gate the writes, and batch them where you can: the Studio 1 project ends with a human reviewing the whole list of proposed action items at once, which is a single, meaningful approval instead of twenty trivial ones.

13
00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:42.880
With the SDK tool runner, you place this gate inside the tool function or in the per-turn hook, because the runner otherwise executes tools as soon as the model asks.

14
00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:47.533
With a manual loop, it sits in your dispatcher, as in the exercise below.
